                             $\\\\text{lcm}(L_i)$?\\nThe order of               
                             $g$ is $\\\\text{lcm}(L_i)$. If $a >               
                             \\\\text{ord}(g)$, then $g^a = g^{a                
                             \\\\pmod{\\\\text{ord}(g)}}$.\\nSo $A =            
                             g^{a                                               
                             \\\\pmod{\\\\text{ord}(g)}}$.\\nThis               
                             means $a \\\\pmod{\\\\text{ord}(g)}$ is            
                             what we can find.\\nOnce we have $a\' =            
                             a \\\\pmod{\\\\text{ord}(g)}$, then                
                             $B^{a\'} = (g^b)^{a\'} = g^{ba\'} =                
                             g^{ba \\\\pmod{\\\\text{ord}(g)}} =                
                             g^{ab \\\\pmod{\\\\text{ord}(g)}} =                
                             C$.\\nSo we just need to find $a                   
                             \\\\pmod{L_i}$ for all                             
                             cycles.\\n\\nLet\'s write a script to              
                             do this.\\nI\'ll need to:\\n1. Read $g,            
                             A, B, c, iv$ from the output.\\n2. Find            
                             cycles of $g$.\\n3. For each cycle,                
                             find $a \\\\pmod L$.\\n4. Compute $C =             
                             B^a$ or $C = A^b$ is not quite right               
                             because I don\'t know $b$.\\nWait, if I            
                             have $a\'$, then $B^{a\'} = (g^b)^{a\'}            
                             = g^{ba\'} = g^{ba                                 
                             \\\\pmod{\\\\text{ord}(g)}}$.\\nSince              
                             $C = g^{ab}$ and $ab                               
                             \\\\pmod{\\\\text{ord}(g)} = (a                    
                             \\\\pmod{\\\\text{ord}(g)}) \\\\cdot b             
                             \\\\pmod{\\\\text{ord}(g)}$, then $C =             
                             g^{(a\' \\\\cdot b)                                
                             \\\\pmod{\\\\text{ord}(g)}}$.\\nWait,              
                             $A = g^a$ means $A$ is the permutation             
                             obtained by applying $g$, $a$                      
